name:           hmssql
version:        0.1.0
synopsis:       Pure Haskell client for Microsoft SQL Server and Azure SQL
description:    hmssql is a native Haskell client for Microsoft SQL Server (2017 and later)
                and Azure SQL Database.  It implements SQL Server's wire protocol directly
                (TDS 7.4 and 8.0), so no ODBC driver or client library is needed.
                .
                The query API resembles postgresql-simple's, except that a query and its
                parameters are one value, so @query conn q params@ becomes @query conn q@.
                A @Query@ is SQL text with its parameters, and a @Monoid@, so SQL
                assembled at runtime from pieces such as @"WHERE id = " <> param x@
                refers to its parameters correctly however it was put together; the
                @sp_executesql@ style with @\@p1@, @\@p2@, ... and a tuple or record of
                parameters works as well.  Rows
                decode into records with a derived @FromSqlRow@, or into tuples, with the
                column types checked before the first row; @queryS@ streams rows from the
                socket through the @streaming@ package, in flat memory for any result
                size.  Several statements go in one round trip, and bulk copy loads a
                table by the protocol's bulk load.
                .
                SQL Server's types are decoded faithfully, @datetimeoffset@ and @decimal@
                included.  Azure SQL's strict encryption (TDS 8.0) is supported alongside
                the older negotiated TLS, with certificate validation by default.
                Interrupting a call with an asynchronous exception (@timeout@,
                @killThread@) cancels the statement on the server and leaves the
                connection usable.
                .
                The design follows hpgsql; the protocol code descends from ms-tds and
                mssql-simple.  hmssql was largely written and tested with AI, directed and
                reviewed by the maintainer; the README at
                <https://github.com/joeyadams/hmssql> says more.
